research=f'''<section class="page-hero dark"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">Research & exploration</div><h1>Ask better questions.<br><span class="light">Engineer new possibilities.</span></h1><p>Exploring the boundary between classical control, machine learning and the practical demands of industrial systems.</p></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap"><div class="research-band"><div class="research-copy"><div class="eyebrow">Control systems × machine learning</div><h2>Neural Network Assisted Model Predictive Control for Nonlinear Industrial Processes</h2><p>Explore Juliet’s research into the relationship between neural networks, model predictive control and nonlinear industrial processes.</p><a class="text-link" href="{PAPER}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read on ResearchGate {ARROW}</a></div><div class="research-art">{RESEARCH_ART}<span class="art-caption">Illustrative response · not a published research result</span></div></div><div class="approach-grid"><div class="step"><span>01 / UNDERSTAND</span><h3>Process behaviour.</h3><p>What does the process do, and where does the model struggle to explain it?</p></div><div class="step"><span>02 / INVESTIGATE</span><h3>Learning and control.</h3><p>How can learned relationships contribute to an engineering model?</p></div><div class="step"><span>03 / EVALUATE</span><h3>Evidence and limits.</h3><p>How should performance, assumptions and validation boundaries be communicated?</p></div></div></div></section>{cta('Have a research question<br>worth exploring?')}'''
page('research.html','Control systems & applied AI research','Explore research by Juliet Ihuoma Uzoeshi into neural network assisted model predictive control for nonlinear industrial processes.',research,'Research')

article_intro='AI can help engineers interpret industrial data, investigate patterns and compare options. The useful starting point is a clearly defined decision and a clear boundary around the control system.'
article=f'''<section class="page-hero dark"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="blog.html">Engineering insights</a><span>/</span><span>Industrial AI</span></div><div class="eyebrow">Engineering perspective · 5 minute read</div><h1>Above the loop:<br><span class="light">where AI fits.</span></h1><p>{article_intro}</p><p class="small">By Juliet Ihuoma Uzoeshi · Updated 4 November 2026</p></div></section><section class="section"><article class="wrap reading"><h2>Start with the decision.</h2><p>A plant already has sensors, controllers, operating procedures and people who understand the process. An AI proposal should begin with a decision that someone needs to make: which signal deserves investigation, which maintenance record is relevant, or which batch parameters are worth evaluating.</p><p>That question defines the data, the model and the interface. It also makes it easier to decide whether machine learning adds value at all.</p><h2>Make the layers explicit.</h2><p>A useful starting architecture separates the physical process, deterministic control, supervisory systems, historical data and decision support. A demonstration dashboard can read example data and present a trend. A production integration needs a more detailed design, including how data arrives, how its quality is checked and what the user is expected to do with the result.</p><blockquote>A useful model output needs context, an owner and a clear next action.</blockquote><h2>Three practical directions.</h2><h3>1. Explore equipment health.</h3><p>Predictive modelling and anomaly detection can help investigate patterns in operating data. The Smart Maintenance Prediction System explores Random Forest and Isolation Forest within a structured data workflow. Its predictions need to be evaluated against representative equipment data before they can support operational decisions.</p><p><a href="work-smart-maintenance.html">Explore the maintenance project ↗</a></p><h3>2. Compare batch performance.</h3><p>Batch history can provide a basis for examining how parameters relate to performance. A recommendation engine can support this analysis, but a proposed parameter is not automatically an approved setpoint. Engineering limits, process requirements and the operator’s role remain part of the design.</p><p><a href="work-batch-optimisation.html">Explore the batch optimisation project ↗</a></p><h3>3. Make process information readable.</h3><p>A dashboard can make changing signals easier to interpret. Good presentation is part of the engineering: display units, time ranges, missing data and the difference between a measured value and an inferred result. The process dashboard project explores this connection between industrial data and the user’s view.</p><p><a href="work-process-intelligence.html">Explore the dashboard project ↗</a></p><h2>Define what the system does not do.</h2><p>For these demonstration projects, the boundary is deliberate: no live plant connection and no automatic changes to operational or safety settings. A real deployment needs its own risk assessment, validation, access design and agreed responsibilities. A working demonstration alone is not evidence of production readiness.</p><h2>Questions to take into the first discussion.</h2><ul><li>What specific decision should the output support?</li><li>What data is available, and how representative is it?</li><li>How will missing values and unusual operating conditions be handled?</li><li>What are the consequences of a false alarm or a missed event?</li><li>How will the result be explained and reviewed?</li><li>What evidence is required before progressing beyond a prototype?</li></ul><p>Industrial AI becomes useful when the engineering problem, the data and the operational context are considered together. That is where the most worthwhile work begins.</p></article></section>{cta('Turn a good question<br>into a practical next step.')}'''
page('blog-ai-control-systems.html','Above the loop: where AI fits in industrial control',article_intro,article,'Insights')
blog=f'''<section class="page-hero dark"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">Engineering insights</div><h1>Notes from<br><span class="light">the intersection.</span></h1><p>Ideas on industrial systems, practical software and the role of intelligence in engineering. Written to make the next conversation more useful.</p></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap article-grid"><a class="article-card" href="blog-ai-control-systems.html"><div class="article-art">{RESEARCH_ART}</div><div class="eyebrow">Industrial AI · Engineering perspective</div><h2>Above the loop: where AI fits in an industrial control system.</h2><p>{article_intro}</p><span class="text-link">Read the perspective {ARROW}</span></a><div><a class="article-card" href="research.html"><div class="eyebrow">Research</div><h3>Neural networks and model predictive control.</h3><p>Explore the published research link and the questions behind the work.</p><span class="text-link">Explore the research {ARROW}</span></a><a class="article-card" href="work.html"><div class="eyebrow">From idea to implementation</div><h3>Look inside the projects.</h3><p>Read about the approach, understand each project’s scope and inspect the source code.</p><span class="text-link">Open the portfolio {ARROW}</span></a><div class="note" id="pid-migration"><span id="time-series"></span>More engineering notes will be added as they are ready. Have a topic you would like to discuss? <a href="contact.html">Start a conversation ↗</a></div></div></div></section>{cta()}'''
page('blog.html','Engineering insights','Read SmartOpsTech perspectives on industrial AI, control systems, software and engineering practice.',blog,'Insights')

